2026-07-14 17:45:36 | DEBUG    | openai._base_client | [-] | Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'headers': {'X-Stainless-Raw-Response': 'true'}, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-61c6230f-a370-46dd-a737-cca3d6718e7b', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'content': 'You are a strict relevance judge for a retrieval system.\n\nORIGINAL USER QUESTION:\nWhat does the research say about the intersection of being both a girl and from a minority racial group when it comes to ASD diagnosis?\n\nRETRIEVED CHUNKS (one per block, indexed):\n--- chunk 0 (source: learned_qa) ---\nQ: Do the disparities in ASD prevalence among different racial and ethnic groups indicate that the apparent stabilization of ASD prevalence between 2014 and 2016 was an illusion?\nA: Higher observed prevalence in minority groups does not mean disparities have disappeared. Instead, it may reflect improvements in recognition in some communities while major inequities remain in the timing of diagnosis, quality of assessment, comorbid condition management, and service access.\n\n--- chunk 1 (source: learned_qa) ---\nQ: How do disparities in ASD diagnosis affect minority communities?\nA: Minority families may experience confusion during the diagnostic process, limited culturally responsive communication, and frustration when parental concerns are dismissed, leading to later identification of ASD symptoms.\n\n--- chunk 2 (source: learned_qa) ---\nQ: How do disparities in ASD diagnosis affect children from minority communities?\nA: Children from minority communities may experience confusion during the diagnostic process, limited culturally responsive communication, and frustration when parental concerns are dismissed, leading to later identification of symptoms.\n\n--- chunk 3 (source: learned_qa) ---\nQ: What are some of the disparities in ASD diagnosis and treatment?\nA: Despite increasing clinical and public attention, the experience of being identified, diagnosed, and treated is not the same for all patients. Gender and racial factors shape who is recognized early, who is overlooked, who receives appropriate services, and who must overcome greater barriers before support becomes available.\n\n--- chunk 4 (source: learned_qa) ---\nQ: Why might girls with ASD be at a higher risk of not receiving a clinical diagnosis?\nA: Girls with ASD may be misdiagnosed or not receive a clinical diagnosis due to differences in the female autism phenotype, which can lead to them being identified later or not at all.\n\n--- chunk 5 (source: learned_qa) ---\nQ: Why are girls with ASD at higher risk of not receiving a clinical diagnosis?\nA: Girls who meet criteria for ASD are at higher risk of not receiving a clinical diagnosis, and the female autism phenotype may play a role in girls being misdiagnosed, diagnosed later, or overlooked.\n\n--- chunk 6 (source: learned_qa) ---\nQ: What are some of the disparities in ASD diagnosis and treatment?\nA: Despite increasing clinical and public attention, the experience of being identified, diagnosed, and treated is not the same for all patients. Gender and racial factors shape who is recognized early, who is overlooked, who receives appropriate services, and who must overcome greater barriers before support becomes available.\n\n--- chunk 7 (source: learned_qa) ---\nQ: Why are girls with ASD at higher risk of not receiving a clinical diagnosis?\nA: Girls with ASD are at higher risk of not receiving a clinical diagnosis due to the female autism phenotype, which may lead to misdiagnosis or the lack of overt symptoms, and the tendency to mask social deficits through camouflaging.\n\n--- chunk 8 (source: learned_qa) ---\nQ: How does the prevalence of ASD vary across different racial and ethnic groups?\nA: ASD occurs in all racial, ethnic, and socioeconomic groups, but its diagnosis is far from uniform across these groups. Caucasian children are consistently identified with ASD more often than black or Hispanic children, and the continued discrepancy may be due to stigma, lack of access to healthcare services, and a patient\'s primary language being one of the barriers.\n\n--- chunk 9 (source: learned_qa) ---\nQ: Do ASD diagnoses occur at the same rate across different racial and ethnic groups?\nA: ASD occurs in all racial, ethnic, and socioeconomic groups, but its diagnosis is far from uniform across these groups. Caucasian children are consistently identified with ASD more often than black or Hispanic children.\n\n--- chunk 10 (source: learned_qa) ---\nQ: Why might girls with ASD be at a higher risk of not receiving a clinical diagnosis?\nA: Girls with ASD may be misdiagnosed or not receive a clinical diagnosis due to differences in the female autism phenotype, which can lead to them being identified later or not at all.\n\n--- chunk 11 (source: learned_qa) ---\nQ: What is the current state of ASD prevalence and identification in the United States?\nA: According to the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention, the prevalence of ASD among children aged 8 years has been increasing, but disparities in diagnosis and access to services persist. Surveillance data suggest some progress in identification across racial and ethnic groups, but significant inequities remain in timing, pathway, quality of diagnosis, and access to services.\n\nYour job: for EACH chunk, decide whether it contains information that\ndirectly helps answer the ORIGINAL USER QUESTION.\n\nA chunk is RELEVANT only if a reader could extract at least one fact from\nit that contributes to answering the question. Tangential mentions of the\ntopic, generic background, or off-topic text are NOT relevant.\n\nThen give an OVERALL verdict:\n- "PASS"    : majority of chunks are relevant AND together they could plausibly support an answer.\n- "PARTIAL" : some chunks are relevant but coverage is thin or incomplete.\n- "FAIL"    : few or no chunks are relevant; the