{
  "content": "Girls with ASD may present differently from the traditional diagnostic picture that was developed largely from male-centered samples. In many cases, girls show fewer overt repetitive behaviors, stronger superficial social imitation, or interests that appear socially acceptable and therefore do not immediately raise concern. Some girls also engage in \u201cmasking\u201d or \u201ccamouflaging,\u201d meaning they consciously or unconsciously hide autistic traits in order to fit social expectations. As a result, they may be identified later, misdiagnosed with anxiety or mood disorders first, or never formally diagnosed at all. [Source: \"../data/html/asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html\"] [Source: \"../data/html/asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html\"]",
