autistic traits in order to fit social expectations. As a result, they may be identified later, misdiagnosed with anxiety or mood disorders first, or never formally diagnosed at all. [Source: “../data/html/asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html”] [Source: “../data/html/asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html”]\n\n--- chunk 2 (source: ..\\data\\html\\asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html, ..\\data\\html\\asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html, ..\\data\\html\\asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html, ..\\data\\html\\asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html) ---\nDisparities in ASD cannot be understood through gender or race alone [Source:../data/html/asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html]. Gender-based underrecognition, especially among girls, and racial inequities in diagnosis and care continue to shape who receives support and who is left behind [Source:../data/html/asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html]. Although surveillance data suggest some progress in identification across racial and ethnic groups [Source:../data/html/asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html], significant inequities remain in timing, pathway, quality of diagnosis, and access to services [Source:../data/html/asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html]. A fairer system will require clinicians, educators, and policymakers to move beyond one-size-fits-all assumptions and recognize the diverse ways autism appears across different populations [Source:../data/html/asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html].\n\nPROPOSED GROUPS:\nGroup 0:\n  chunk_index=0  sentence="However, higher observed prevalence does not mean disparities have disappeared."\n  chunk_index=2  sentence="Although surveillance data suggest some progress in identification across racial and ethnic groups,"\n\nGroup 1:\n  chunk_index=0  sentence="Instead, it may reflect improvements in recognition in some communities while major inequities remain in the timing of diagnosis, quality of assessment, comorbid condition management, and service access."\n  chunk_index=2  sentence="Although surveillance data suggest some progress in identification across racial and ethnic groups, significant inequities remain in timing, pathway, quality of diagnosis, and access to services."\n\nGroup 2:\n  chunk_index=0  sentence="In other words, being counted is not the same as being equitably served."\n  chunk_index=2  sentence="Gender-based underrecognition, especially among girls, and racial inequities in diagnosis and care continue to shape who receives support and who is left behind."\n\nGroup 3:\n  chunk_index=1  sentence="Some girls also engage in ‘masking’ or ‘camouflaging,’ meaning they consciously or unconsciously hide autistic traits in order to fit social expectations."\n  chunk_index=2  sentence="A fairer system will require clinicians, educators, and policymakers to move beyond one-size-fits-all assumptions and recognize the diverse ways autism appears across different populations."\n\nA group is CONFIRMED only if:\n- all sentences have the same subject\n- all sentences make the same claim\n- all sentences have the same meaning\n- removing duplicate copies would NOT lose unique information\n\nIMPORTANT:\n- Same topic is NOT enough.\n- Related ideas are NOT enough.\n- Similar wording is NOT enough.\n- Advice and explanation are NOT automatically the same fact.\n- General statements and specific instructions are NOT redundant.\n- If one sentence adds a unique detail, the group is REJECTED.\n- If the subjects are different real-world entities, the group is REJECTED.\n- If meanings conflict or one sentence negates another, the group is REJECTED.\n\nBe STRICT.\nBe CONSERVATIVE.\nIf unsure, REJECT the group.\n\nGOOD:\n"ASDs vary motor speed by controlling voltage and frequency."\n"An ASD regulates motor speed by adjusting frequency and voltage."\nVerdict: CONFIRMED\n\nBAD:\n"ASD patients may experience sensory overload."\n"Healthcare providers should reduce loud noises."\nVerdict: REJECTED\nReason: related topic, but different facts.\n\nBAD:\n"Managing ASD patients requires individualized care."\n"Reduce bright lights and loud sounds."\nVerdict: REJECTED\nReason: general guidance versus specific instruction.\n\nBAD:\n"ASDs reduce energy costs and mechanical wear."\n"ASDs reduce energy costs."\nVerdict: REJECTED\nReason: one sentence contains unique information.\n\nOUTPUT FORMAT:\nReturn ONLY a JSON array.\nOne object per proposed group, in the same order as the input groups.\n\n[\n  {\n    "group_index": 0,\n    "verdict": "CONFIRMED",\n    "reason": "Same subject and same factual claim."\n  },\n  {\n    "group_index": 1,\n    "verdict": "REJECTED",\n    "reason": "Related topic but different facts."\n  }\n]\n\nBAD OUTPUTS:\n- Python code\n- Pseudocode\n- Markdown\n- Explanations outside JSON\n- Text before or after the JSON array\n\nThe first character of your response must be \'[\'.\nYour response will be parsed directly using json.loads().\nInvalid JSON will cause failure.\n\nReturn ONLY the JSON array.\n', 'role': 'system'}], 'model': 'llama-3.1-8b-instruct', 'max_completion_tokens': 1024, 'stream': False, 'temperature': 0.0}}
