isorder definition, epidemiology, causes, and clinical evaluation.pdf]. While the differences appear to be decreasing, the continued discrepancy may be due to stigma, lack of access to healthcare services, and a patient\\u2019s primary language being one other than English [Source:../data/pdfs/Autism spectrum disorder definition, epidemiology, causes, and clinical evaluation.pdf]."\n\n[Source: "../data/pdfs/Autism spectrum disorder definition, epidemiology, causes, and clinical evaluation.pdf, ../data/pdfs/Autism spectrum disorder definition, epidemiology, causes, and clinical evaluation.pdf"]\n"ASD is more common in males (22,23) but in a recent meta-analysis (24), true male-to-female ratio is closer to 3:1 than the previously reported 4:1, though this study was not done using the DSM-5 criteria. This study also suggested that girls who meet criteria for ASD are at higher risk of not receiving a clinical diagnosis. The female autism phenotype may play a role in girls being misdiagnosed, diagnosed later, or overlooked. Not only are females less likely to present with overt symptoms, they are more likely to mask their social deficits through a process called \\u2018camouflaging\\u2019, further hindering a timely diagnosis (25). Likewise, gender biases and stereotypes of ASD as a male disorder could also hamper diagnoses in girls (26)."', 'role': 'system'}], 'model': 'llama-3.1-8b-instruct', 'max_completion_tokens': 2048, 'stream': False, 'temperature': 0.1}}
