2026-07-14 18:20:59 | DEBUG    | openai._base_client | [-] | Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'headers': {'X-Stainless-Raw-Response': 'true'}, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-08f7d4fa-e8c5-4460-ab4c-a5f14e5b15d5', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'content': 'You are performing sentence-level redundancy annotation.\n\nYour task:\nIdentify sentences from DIFFERENT chunks that express the SAME FACT.\n\nIMPORTANT:\nTwo sentences are redundant ONLY if they communicate essentially the SAME information.\n\nThe sentences must match on:\n- subject\n- claim\n- meaning\n\nBeing about the SAME TOPIC is NOT enough.\n\nYou are NOT writing software.\nYou are NOT generating Python.\nYou are NOT solving a coding task.\nYou are ONLY selecting matching factual statements.\n\nReturn ONLY a JSON array.\n\nCHUNKS:\n--- chunk 0 (source: ..\\data\\html\\asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html, ..\\data\\html\\asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html, ..\\data\\html\\asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html) ---\nGirls with ASD may present differently from the traditional diagnostic picture that was developed largely from male-centered samples. In many cases, girls show fewer overt repetitive behaviors, stronger superficial social imitation, or interests that appear socially acceptable and therefore do not immediately raise concern. Some girls also engage in “masking” or “camouflaging,” meaning they consciously or unconsciously hide autistic traits in order to fit social expectations. As a result, they may be identified later, misdiagnosed with anxiety or mood disorders first, or never formally diagnosed at all   .\n\n--- chunk 1 (source: ..\\data\\html\\asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html, ..\\data\\html\\asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html, ..\\data\\html\\asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html, ..\\data\\html\\asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html, ..\\data\\html\\asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html) ---\nDisparities in ASD cannot be understood through gender or race alone     . Gender-based underrecognition, especially among girls, and racial inequities in diagnosis and care continue to shape who receives support and who is left behind     . Although surveillance data suggest some progress in identification across racial and ethnic groups, significant inequities remain in timing, pathway, quality of diagnosis, and access to services     . A fairer system will require clinicians, educators, and policymakers to move beyond one-size-fits-all assumptions and recognize the diverse ways autism appears across different populations     .\n\n--- chunk 2 (source: ..\\data\\html\\asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html, ..\\data\\html\\asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html, ..\\data\\html\\asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html, ..\\data\\html\\asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html, ..\\data\\html\\asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html, ..\\data\\html\\asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html) ---\nMore recent CDC surveillance data show a different pattern in prevalence estimates, with ASD prevalence in recent samples being lower among White children than among several other racial and ethnic groups, including Black, Hispanic, Asian/Pacific Islander, and American Indian/Alaska Native children   . However, higher observed prevalence does not mean disparities have disappeared   . Instead, it may reflect improvements in recognition in some communities while major inequities remain in the timing of diagnosis, quality of assessment, comorbid condition management, and service access   . In other words, being counted is not the same as being equitably served   .\n\nWHAT COUNTS AS REDUNDANT:\n- Same fact\n- Same subject\n- Same meaning\n- Paraphrases are allowed\n\nGOOD EXAMPLE:\nChunk 0:\n"ASD affects 1 in 36 children."\n\nChunk 1:\n"Approximately 1 in 36 children have autism."\n\nThese ARE redundant because they express the same fact.\n\nANOTHER GOOD EXAMPLE:\nChunk 0:\n"ASDs regulate motor speed by changing voltage and frequency."\n\nChunk 1:\n"An ASD controls motor speed through voltage and frequency adjustment."\n\nThese ARE redundant.\n\nWHAT IS NOT REDUNDANT:\n\nNOT REDUNDANT — SAME TOPIC BUT DIFFERENT FACTS:\nChunk 0:\n"ASD patients may experience sensory overload."\n\nChunk 1:\n"Healthcare providers should reduce loud noises."\n\nThese are RELATED but NOT redundant.\n\nNOT REDUNDANT — DIFFERENT SUBJECTS:\nChunk 0:\n"Dawood\'s policy includes accidents."\n\nChunk 1:\n"Umair\'s policy includes accidents."\n\nDifferent subjects = NOT redundant.\n\nNOT REDUNDANT — GENERAL VS SPECIFIC:\nChunk 0:\n"Managing ASD patients requires individualized care."\n\nChunk 1:\n"Reduce bright lights and loud sounds."\n\nThese are NOT the same fact.\n\nIMPORTANT RULES:\n- A group MUST contain sentences from AT LEAST TWO DIFFERENT chunks.\n- Never group sentences from only one chunk.\n- One shared fact = one group.\n- Copy sentences EXACTLY as written.\n- Do not rewrite sentences.\n- Be CONSERVATIVE.\n- If unsure, DO NOT group.\n- It is BETTER to miss a redundancy than to create a false match.\n\nIf nothing is redundant, return:\n[]\n\nOUTPUT FORMAT:\n[\n  {\n    "members": [\n      {\n        "chunk_index": 0,\n        "sentence": "Exact sentence from chunk."\n      },\n      {\n        "chunk_index": 1,\n        "sentence": "Matching sentence from another chunk."\n      }\n    ]\n  }\n]\n\nBAD OUTPUTS:\n- Python\n- Pseudocode\n- Explanations\n- Markdown\n- Comments\n- Any text outside the JSON\n\nThe first character of your response must be \'[\'\n\nYour response will be parsed directly using json.loads().\nInvalid JSON will cause failure.\n\nReturn ONLY the JSON array.\n', 'role': 'system'}], 'model': 'llama-3.1-8b-instruct', 'max_completion_tokens': 2048, 'stream': False, 'temperature': 0.1}}
