oups, but its diagnosis is far from uniform across these groups. Caucasian children are consistently identified with ASD more often than black or Hispanic children.\n\n[6] Surveillance data suggest some progress in identification across racial and ethnic groups, but significant inequities remain in timing, pathway, quality of diagnosis, and access to services\n\n[7] Racial and ethnic disparities in ASD diagnosis can result in delays, misclassification, or incomplete evaluation pathways, highlighting the need for culturally responsive assessment frameworks and provider training to address these inequities.\n\n[8] Girls with ASD may present differently from the traditional diagnostic picture that was developed largely from male-centered samples. In many cases, girls show fewer overt repetitive behaviors, stronger superficial social imitation, or interests that appear socially acceptable and therefore do not immediately raise concern. Some girls also engage in “masking” or “camouflaging,” meaning they consciously or unconsciously hide autistic traits in order to fit social expectations. As a result, they may be identified later, misdiagnosed with anxiety or mood disorders first, or never formally diagnosed at all\n\n[9] Disparities in ASD cannot be understood through gender or race alone [Source:../data/html/asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html] [Source:../data/html/asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html] [Source:../data/html/asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html] [Source:../data/html/asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html] [Source:../data/html/asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html]. Gender-based underrecognition, especially among girls, and racial inequities in diagnosis and care continue to shape who receives support and who is left behind [Source:../data/html/asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html] [Source:../data/html/asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html] [Source:../data/html/asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html] [Source:../data/html/asd_disparities_gender_