[7] Racial and ethnic disparities in ASD diagnosis can result in delays, misclassification, or incomplete evaluation pathways, highlighting the need for culturally responsive assessment frameworks and provider training to address these inequities.

[8] Girls with ASD may present differently from the traditional diagnostic picture that was developed largely from male-centered samples. In many cases, girls show fewer overt repetitive behaviors, stronger superficial social imitation, or interests that appear socially acceptable and therefore do not immediately raise concern. Some girls also engage in “masking” or “camouflaging,” meaning they consciously or unconsciously hide autistic traits in order to fit social expectations. As a result, they may be identified later, misdiagnosed with anxiety or mood disorders first, or never formally diagnosed at all

[9] Disparities in ASD cannot be understood through gender or race alone [Source:../data/html/asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html] [Source:../data/html/asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html] [Source:../data/html/asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html] [Source:../data/html/asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html] [Source:../data/html/asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html]. Gender-based underrecognition, especially among girls, and racial inequities in diagnosis and care continue to shape who receives support and who is left behind [Source:../data/html/asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html] [Source:../data/html/asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html] [Source:../data/html/asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html] [Source:../data/html/asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html] [Source:../data/html/asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html]. Although surveillance data suggest some progress in identification across racial and ethnic groups, significant inequities remain in timing, pathway, quality of diagnosis, and access to services [Source:../data/html/asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html] [Source:../data/html/asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html] [Source:../data/html/asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html] [Source:../data/html/asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html] [Source:../data/html/asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html]. A fairer system will require clinicians, educators, and policymakers to move beyond one-size-fits-all assumptions and recognize the diverse ways autism appears across different populations [Source:../data/html/asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html] [Source:../data/html/asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html] [Source:../data/html/asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html] [Source:../data/html/asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html] [Source:../data/html/asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html].

[10] More recent CDC surveillance data show a different pattern in prevalence estimates, with ASD prevalence in recent samples being lower among White children than among several other racial and ethnic groups, including Black, Hispanic, Asian/Pacific Islander, and American Indian/Alaska Native children. However, higher observed prevalence does not mean disparities have disappeared. Instead, it may reflect improvements in recognition in some communities while major inequities remain in the timing of diagnosis, quality of assessment, comorbid condition management, and service access. In other words, being counted is not the same as being equitably served.

[11] ASD is more common in males (22,23) but in a recent meta-analysis (24), true male-to-female ratio is closer to 3:1 than the previously reported 4:1, though this study was not done using the DSM-5 criteria. This study also suggested that girls who meet criteria for ASD are at higher risk of not receiving a clinical diagnosis. The female autism phenotype may play a role in girls being misdiagnosed, diagnosed later, or overlooked. Not only are females less likely to present with overt symptoms, they are more likely to mask their social deficits through a process called ‘camouflaging’, further hindering a timely diagnosis (25). Likewise, gender biases and stereotypes of ASD as a male disorder could also hamper diagnoses in girls (26).

DRAFT ANSWER (working material — not the source of truth):
Research suggests that girls from minority racial groups may face unique challenges in ASD diagnosis. Girls with ASD may be misdiagnosed or not receive a clinical diagnosis due to differences in the female autism phenotype, which can lead to them being identified later or not at all [4]. This may be exacerbated by racial inequities in diagnosis and care, as well as gender biases and stereotypes of ASD as a male disorder [11]. As a result, girls who meet criteria for ASD are at higher risk of not receiving a clinical diagnosis, and the female autism phenotype may play a role in girls being misdiagnosed, diagnosed later, or overlooked [4, 11].

