ver, this may reflect improvements in recognition in some communities, while major inequities remain in the timing of diagnosis and service access.\n\n--- chunk 8 (source: learned_qa) ---\nQ: How does the prevalence of Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD) vary across different racial and ethnic groups in the US?\nA: More recent CDC surveillance data show a different pattern in prevalence estimates, with ASD prevalence in recent samples being lower among White children than among several other racial and ethnic groups, including Black, Hispanic, Asian/Pacific Islander, and American Indian/Alaska Native children.\n\n--- chunk 9 (source: learned_qa) ---\nQ: What are the racial and ethnic disparities in Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD) diagnosis?\nA: ASD prevalence is lower among White children than among several other racial and ethnic groups, including Black, Hispanic, Asian/Pacific Islander, and American Indian/Alaska Native children. However, this may reflect improvements in recognition in some communities, while major inequities remain in diagnosis timing, assessment quality, and service access.\n\n--- chunk 10 (source: learned_qa) ---\nQ: What are the racial and ethnic disparities in Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD) diagnosis?\nA: ASD prevalence is lower among White children than among several other racial and ethnic groups, including Black, Hispanic, Asian/Pacific Islander, and American Indian/Alaska Native children. However, this may reflect improvements in recognition in some communities, while major inequities remain in the timing of diagnosis and service access.\n\n--- chunk 11 (source: learned_qa) ---\nQ: How does the prevalence of Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD) vary across different racial and ethnic groups?\nA: More recent CDC surveillance data show a different pattern in prevalence estimates, with ASD prevalence in recent samples being lower among White children than among several other racial and ethnic groups, including Black, Hispanic, Asian/Pacific Islander, an