2026-07-14 18:24:11 | DEBUG    | openai._base_client | [-] | Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'headers': {'X-Stainless-Raw-Response': 'true'}, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-27ae5f3d-3cf5-4c19-952f-fdc8f7309f2b', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'content': 'You are a strict relevance judge for a retrieval system.\n\nORIGINAL USER QUESTION:\nDid Black and Hispanic children historically receive more or fewer ASD diagnoses than White children, and has that changed recently?\n\nRETRIEVED CHUNKS (one per block, indexed):\n--- chunk 0 (source: ..\\data\\pdfs\\Autism spectrum disorder definition, epidemiology, causes, and clinical evaluation.pdf) ---\nASD occurs in all racial, ethnic, and socioeconomic groups, but its diagnosis is far from uniform across these groups. Caucasian children are consistently identified with ASD more often than black or Hispanic children (6). While the differences appear to be decreasing, the continued discrepancy may be due to stigma, lack of access to healthcare services, and a patient’s primary language being one other than English.\n\n--- chunk 1 (source: ..\\data\\html\\asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html) ---\nMore recent CDC surveillance data show a different pattern in prevalence estimates, with ASD prevalence in recent samples being lower among White children than among several other racial and ethnic groups, including Black, Hispanic, Asian/Pacific Islander, and American Indian/Alaska Native children. However, higher observed prevalence does not mean disparities have disappeared. Instead, it may reflect improvements in recognition in some communities while major inequities remain in the timing of diagnosis, quality of assessment, comorbid condition management, and service access. In other words, being counted is not the same as being equitably served.\n\n--- chunk 2 (source: ..\\data\\html\\asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html) ---\nRacial and ethnic disparities in ASD are also complex. Older research often showed that Black and Hispanic children were less likely than White children to have a documented autism diagnosis in service records and were more likely to face delays, misclassification, or incomplete evaluation pathways. Barriers such as language differences, stigma, limited specialist access, provider bias, transportation problems, and weak continuity of care contributed to these gaps.\n\n--- chunk 3 (source: ..\\data\\html\\asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html) ---\nDisparities in ASD cannot be understood through gender or race alone. Gender-based underrecognition, especially among girls, and racial inequities in diagnosis and care continue to shape who receives support and who is left behind. Although surveillance data suggest some progress in identification across racial and ethnic groups, significant inequities remain in timing, pathway, quality of diagnosis, and access to services. A fairer system will require clinicians, educators, and policymakers to move beyond one-size-fits-all assumptions and recognize the diverse ways autism appears across different populations.\n\n--- chunk 4 (source: ..\\data\\html\\asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html) ---\nRacial and ethnic disparities in ASD are also complex. Older research often showed that Black and Hispanic children were less likely than White children to have a documented autism diagnosis in service records and were more likely to face delays, misclassification, or incomplete evaluation pathways. Barriers such as language differences, stigma, limited specialist access, provider bias, transportation problems, and weak continuity of care contributed to these gaps.\n\n--- chunk 5 (source: ..\\data\\pdfs\\Autism spectrum disorder definition, epidemiology, causes, and clinical evaluation.pdf) ---\n10. Hartley-McAndrew M, Mertz J, Hoffman M, et al. Rates of autism spectrum disorder diagnosis under the DSM- 5 criteria compared to DSM-IV-TR criteria in a hospital- based clinic. Pediatr Neurol 2016;57:34-8.\n\n--- chunk 6 (source: ..\\data\\html\\asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html) ---\nWiggins, L. D., et al. Disparities in Documented Diagnoses of Autism Spectrum Disorder Based on Demographic, Individual, and Service Factors. CDC / Autism Research, 2020.\n\n--- chunk 7 (source: ..\\data\\pdfs\\Autism spectrum disorder definition, epidemiology, causes, and clinical evaluation.pdf) ---\n13. Gibbs V, Aldridge F, Chandler F, et al. Brief report: an exploratory study comparing diagnostic outcomes for autism spectrum disorders under DSM-IV-TR with the proposed DSM-5 revision. J Autism Dev Disord 2012;42:1750-6.\n\n--- chunk 8 (source: ..\\data\\html\\asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html) ---\nWiggins, L. D., et al. Disparities in Documented Diagnoses of Autism Spectrum Disorder Based on Demographic, Individual, and Service Factors. CDC / Autism Research, 2020.\n\n--- chunk 9 (source: ..\\data\\html\\asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html) ---\nDisparities in ASD cannot be understood through gender or race alone. Gender-based underrecognition, especially among girls, and racial inequities in diagnosis and care continue to shape who receives support and who is left behind. Although surveillance data suggest some progress in identification across racial and ethnic groups, significant inequities remain in timing, pathway, quality of diagnosis, and access to services. A fairer system will require clinicians, educators, and policymakers to move beyond one-size-fits-all assumptions and recognize the diverse ways autism appears across different populations.\n\n--- chunk 10 (source: ..\\data\\html\\asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html) ---\nRacial and ethnic disparities in ASD are also complex. Older research often showed that Black and Hispanic children were less likely than White children to have a documented autism diagnosis in service records and were more likely to face delays, misclassification, or incomplete evaluation pathways. Barriers such as language differences, stigma, limited specialist access, provider bias, transportation problems, and weak continuity of care contributed to these gaps.\n\n--- chunk 11 (source: ..\\data\\html\\asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html) ---\nReducing disparities among ASD patients requires action at multiple levels. Screening tools and clinical training should reflect a broader range of autistic presentations, especially in girls and culturally diverse populations. Providers should be trained to recognize masking, subtle social communication differences, and the ways autism may be expressed differently across communities. Diagnostic pathways should include interpreters, culturally responsive family communication, and patient navigation support for families facing practical barriers.\n\nYour job: for EACH chunk, decide whether it contains information that\ndirectly helps answer the ORIGINAL USER QUESTION.\n\nA chunk is RELEVANT only if a reader could extract at least one fact from\nit that contributes to answering the question. Tangential mentions of the\ntopic, generic background, or off-topic text are NOT relevant.\n\nThen give an OVERALL verdict:\n- "PASS"    : majority of chunks are relevant AND together they could plausibly support an answer.\n- "PARTIAL" : some chunks are relevant but coverage is thin or incomplete.\n- "FAIL"    : few or no chunks are relevant; the agent should retry with a different query.\n\nReturn ONLY a JSON object (no markdown, no prose, no code fences) with this exact shape:\n\n{\n  "verdict": "PASS" | "PARTIAL" | "FAIL",\n  "per_chunk": [\n    {"index": 0, "relevant": true,  "reason": "<one short sentence>"},\n    {"index": 1, "relevant": false, "reason": "<one short sentence>"}\n  ],\n  "overall_reason": "<one or two sentences>"\n}\n', 'role': 'system'}], 'model': 'llama-3.1-8b-instruct', 'max_completion_tokens': 1024, 'stream': False, 'temperature': 0.0}}
