2026-07-14 18:31:40 | DEBUG    | openai._base_client | [-] | Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'headers': {'X-Stainless-Raw-Response': 'true'}, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-2ac58043-ddea-4926-abe5-66977d470e83', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'content': 'You are a strict relevance judge for a retrieval system.\n\nORIGINAL USER QUESTION:\nWhat does masking mean in the context of autism, and which population is most affected by it?\n\nRETRIEVED CHUNKS (one per block, indexed):\n--- chunk 0 (source: learned_qa) ---\nQ: What is masking in the context of autism, and how does it affect girls with ASD?\nA: Girls with Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD) may engage in \'masking\' or \'camouflaging,\' hiding autistic traits to fit social expectations. This can lead to delayed or missed diagnoses, as they may be identified later or misdiagnosed with anxiety or mood disorders.\n\n--- chunk 1 (source: learned_qa) ---\nQ: What is masking in the context of autism, and which population is most affected?\nA: Girls with Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD) may engage in \'masking\' or \'camouflaging,\' hiding autistic traits to fit social expectations. This can lead to delayed or missed diagnoses. Girls from culturally diverse populations are particularly at risk of being overlooked.\n\n--- chunk 2 (source: learned_qa) ---\nQ: How does the presentation of Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD) differ between girls and boys?\nA: Girls with ASD may present differently from the traditional diagnostic picture, showing fewer overt repetitive behaviors, stronger superficial social imitation, or interests that appear socially acceptable. Some girls also engage in “masking” or “camouflaging,” hiding autistic traits to fit social expectations.\n\n--- chunk 3 (source: learned_qa) ---\nQ: How does the presentation of Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD) differ between girls and boys?\nA: Girls with ASD may present differently from the traditional diagnostic picture, showing fewer overt repetitive behaviors, stronger superficial social imitation, or interests that appear socially acceptable. Some girls also engage in \'masking\' or \'camouflaging,\' hiding autistic traits to fit social expectations.\n\n--- chunk 4 (source: learned_qa) ---\nQ: What is masking in the context of autism, and which population is most affected?\nA: Girls with Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD) may engage in \'masking\' or \'camouflaging,\' hiding autistic traits to fit social expectations. This can lead to delayed or missed diagnoses. Girls from culturally diverse populations are particularly at risk of being overlooked.\n\n--- chunk 5 (source: learned_qa) ---\nQ: What is masking in the context of autism, and how does it affect girls with ASD?\nA: Girls with Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD) may engage in \'masking\' or \'camouflaging,\' hiding autistic traits to fit social expectations. This can lead to delayed or missed diagnoses, as they may be identified later or misdiagnosed with anxiety or mood disorders.\n\n--- chunk 6 (source: learned_qa) ---\nQ: How can disparities among ASD patients be reduced?\nA: Reducing disparities among ASD patients requires action at multiple levels, including screening tools and clinical training that reflect a broader range of autistic presentations, especially in girls and culturally diverse populations. Providers should be trained to recognize masking, subtle social communication differences, and the ways autism may be expressed differently across communities.\n\n--- chunk 7 (source: learned_qa) ---\nQ: How does the presentation of Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD) differ between girls and boys?\nA: Girls with ASD may present differently from the traditional diagnostic picture, showing fewer overt repetitive behaviors, stronger superficial social imitation, or interests that appear socially acceptable. Some girls also engage in “masking” or “camouflaging,” hiding autistic traits to fit social expectations.\n\n--- chunk 8 (source: learned_qa) ---\nQ: What are the estimated prevalence rates of Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD) globally and in the United States?\nA: The World Health Organization estimates the international prevalence of ASD at 0.76%, while the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention estimates that about 1.68% of children aged 8 years in the United States are diagnosed with ASD. This represents a significant increase in prevalence rates in the US, with the Autism and Developmental Disabilities Monitoring Network estimating a more than doubling of cases between 2000-2002 and 2010-2012.\n\n--- chunk 9 (source: learned_qa) ---\nQ: What are the estimated prevalence rates of Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD) globally and in the United States?\nA: The World Health Organization estimates the international prevalence of ASD at 0.76%, accounting for approximately 16% of the global child population. The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention estimates about 1.68% of US children aged 8 years are diagnosed with ASD, while parent-reported ASD diagnoses in 2016 averaged slightly higher at 2.5%.\n\n--- chunk 10 (source: learned_qa) ---\nQ: How does the prevalence of Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD) vary across different racial and ethnic groups?\nA: More recent CDC surveillance data show a different pattern in prevalence estimates, with ASD prevalence in recent samples being lower among White children than among several other racial and ethnic groups, including Black, Hispanic, Asian/Pacific Islander, and American Indian/Alaska Native children.\n\n--- chunk 11 (source: learned_qa) ---\nQ: What are the estimated prevalence rates of Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD) globally and in the United States?\nA: The World Health Organization estimates the international prevalence of ASD at 0.76%, while the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention estimates that about 1.68% of children aged 8 years in the United States are diagnosed with ASD, which is approximately 1 in 59 children. This prevalence rate in the US more than doubled between 2000-2002 and 2010-2012.\n\n--- chunk 12 (source: learned_qa) ---\nQ: What is masking in the context of autism, and how does it affect girls with ASD?\nA: Girls with Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD) may engage in \'masking\' or \'camouflaging,\' hiding autistic traits to fit social expectations. This can lead to delayed or missed diagnoses, as they may be identified later or misdiagnosed with anxiety or mood disorders.\n\n--- chunk 13 (source: learned_qa) ---\nQ: What is masking in the context of autism, and which population is most affected?\nA: Girls with Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD) may engage in \'masking\' or \'camouflaging,\' hiding autistic traits to fit social expectations. This can lead to delayed or missed diagnoses. Girls from culturally diverse populations are particularly at risk of being overlooked.\n\n--- chunk 14 (source: learned_qa) ---\nQ: How does the presentation of Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD) differ between girls and boys?\nA: Girls with ASD may present differently from the traditional diagnostic picture, showing fewer overt repetitive behaviors, stronger superficial social imitation, or interests that appear socially acceptable. Some girls also engage in “masking” or “camouflaging,” hiding autistic traits to fit social expectations.\n\n--- chunk 15 (source: learned_qa) ---\nQ: How does the presentation of Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD) differ between girls and boys?\nA: Girls with ASD may present differently from the traditional diagnostic picture, showing fewer overt repetitive behaviors, stronger superficial social imitation, or interests that appear socially acceptable. Some girls also engage in \'masking\' or \'camouflaging,\' hiding autistic traits to fit social expectations.\n\n--- chunk 16 (source: learned_qa) ---\nQ: What is masking in the context of autism, and which population is most affected?\nA: Girls with Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD