2026-07-14 18:32:03 | DEBUG    | openai._base_client | [-] | Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'headers': {'X-Stainless-Raw-Response': 'true'}, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-1b0ae1a5-1479-46ad-8e12-c43697bd00ce', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'content': 'You are a strict relevance judge for a retrieval system.\n\nORIGINAL USER QUESTION:\nWhat does masking mean in the context of autism, and which population is most affected by it?\n\nRETRIEVED CHUNKS (one per block, indexed):\n--- chunk 0 (source: ..\\data\\html\\asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html) ---\nGirls with ASD may present differently from the traditional diagnostic picture that was developed largely from male-centered samples. In many cases, girls show fewer overt repetitive behaviors, stronger superficial social imitation, or interests that appear socially acceptable and therefore do not immediately raise concern. Some girls also engage in “masking” or “camouflaging,” meaning they consciously or unconsciously hide autistic traits in order to fit social expectations. As a result, they may be identified later, misdiagnosed with anxiety or mood disorders first, or never formally diagnosed at all.\n\n--- chunk 1 (source: ..\\data\\word\\ASD_Patient_Management_Handbook.docx) ---\nAutism Spectrum Disorder is a neurodevelopmental condition characterized by challenges in social communication, restricted interests, repetitive behaviors, and sensory sensitivities. ASD exists on a spectrum, meaning symptoms and support needs vary significantly between individuals.\n\n--- chunk 2 (source: C:\\Users\\LOQ\\Desktop\\Projects\\RAG-work\\data\\json\\asd_information.json) ---\nAutism Spectrum Disorder (ASD) is a neurodevelopmental condition characterized by differences in communication, social interaction, behavior patterns, and sensory processing.\n\n--- chunk 3 (source: ..\\data\\html\\asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html) ---\nAutism spectrum disorder (ASD) is a neurodevelopmental condition associated with differences in social communication, restricted interests, repetitive behaviors, and sensory processing. Although ASD has received increasing clinical and public attention, the experience of being identified, diagnosed, and treated is not the same for all patients. Gender and racial factors shape who is recognized early, who is overlooked, who receives appropriate services, and who must overcome greater barriers before support becomes available. These disparities matter because delayed identification can postpone therapy, school accommodations, family guidance, and access to specialized care.\n\n--- chunk 4 (source: ..\\data\\html\\asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html) ---\nReducing disparities among ASD patients requires action at multiple levels. Screening tools and clinical training should reflect a broader range of autistic presentations, especially in girls and culturally diverse populations. Providers should be trained to recognize masking, subtle social communication differences, and the ways autism may be expressed differently across communities. Diagnostic pathways should include interpreters, culturally responsive family communication, and patient navigation support for families facing practical barriers.\n\n--- chunk 5 (source: ..\\data\\html\\asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html) ---\nGirls with ASD may present differently from the traditional diagnostic picture that was developed largely from male-centered samples. In many cases, girls show fewer overt repetitive behaviors, stronger superficial social imitation, or interests that appear socially acceptable and therefore do not immediately raise concern. Some girls also engage in “masking” or “camouflaging,” meaning they consciously or unconsciously hide autistic traits in order to fit social expectations. As a result, they may be identified later, misdiagnosed with anxiety or mood disorders first, or never formally diagnosed at all.\n\n--- chunk 6 (source: ..\\data\\html\\asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html) ---\nThe most overlooked patients are often those who sit at the intersection of both kinds of disadvantage, such as autistic girls from Black, Hispanic, Indigenous, or other underserved communities. These patients may be less likely to fit dominant stereotypes of autism and more likely to encounter structural barriers in healthcare and education. A clinician who expects autism to appear mainly in boys, and who also carries cultural assumptions about family communication or behavior, may miss the diagnosis entirely.\n\n--- chunk 7 (source: ..\\data\\html\\asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html) ---\nAutism spectrum disorder (ASD) is a neurodevelopmental condition associated with differences in social communication, restricted interests, repetitive behaviors, and sensory processing. Although ASD has received increasing clinical and public attention, the experience of being identified, diagnosed, and treated is not the same for all patients. Gender and racial factors shape who is recognized early, who is overlooked, who receives appropriate services, and who must overcome greater barriers before support becomes available. These disparities matter because delayed identification can postpone therapy, school accommodations, family guidance, and access to specialized care.\n\n--- chunk 8 (source: ..\\data\\html\\asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html) ---\nWiggins, L. D., et al. Disparities in Documented Diagnoses of Autism Spectrum Disorder Based on Demographic, Individual, and Service Factors. CDC / Autism Research, 2020.\n\n--- chunk 9 (source: ..\\data\\html\\asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html) ---\nCenters for Disease Control and Prevention. Prevalence and Early Identification of Autism Spectrum Disorder Among Children Aged 8 Years — Autism and Developmental Disabilities Monitoring Network, 16 Sites, United States, 2022. CDC MMWR, 2025.\n\n--- chunk 10 (source: ..\\data\\html\\asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html) ---\nDisparities in ASD cannot be understood through gender or race alone. Gender-based underrecognition, especially among girls, and racial inequities in diagnosis and care continue to shape who receives support and who is left behind. Although surveillance data suggest some progress in identification across racial and ethnic groups, significant inequities remain in timing, pathway, quality of diagnosis, and access to services. A fairer system will require clinicians, educators, and policymakers to move beyond one-size-fits-all assumptions and recognize the diverse ways autism appears across different populations.\n\n--- chunk 11 (source: ..\\data\\pdfs\\Autism spectrum disorder definition, epidemiology, causes, and clinical evaluation.pdf) ---\nmedical disorders and disabilities in children with autistic disorder: A population-based study. Autism 2004;8:49-60. 84. Curtin C, Anderson SE, Must A, et al. The prevalence of obesity in children with autism: a secondary data analysis using nationally representative data from the National Survey of Children’s Health. BMC Pediatr 2010;10:11.\n\n--- chunk 12 (source: ..\\data\\html\\asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html) ---\nGirls with ASD may present differently from the traditional diagnostic picture that was developed largely from male-centered samples. In many cases, girls show fewer overt repetitive behaviors, stronger superficial social imitation, or interests that appear socially acceptable and therefore do not immediately raise concern. Some girls also engage in “masking” or “camouflaging,” meaning they consciously or unconsciously hide autistic traits in order to fit social expectations. As a result, they may be identified later, misdiagnosed with anxiety or mood disorders first, or never formally diagnosed at all.\n\n--- chunk 13 (source: ..\\data\\word\\ASD_Patient_Management_Handbook.docx) ---\nAutism Spectrum Disorder is a neurodevelopmental condition characterized by challenges in social communication, restricted interests, repetitive behaviors, and sensory sensitivities. ASD exists on a spectrum, meaning symptoms and support needs vary significantly between individuals.\n\n--- chunk 14 (source: C:\\Users\\LOQ\\Desktop\\Projects\\RAG-work\\data\\json\\asd_information.json) ---\nAutism Spectrum Disorder (ASD) is a neurodevelopmental condition characterized by differences in communication, social interaction, behavior patterns, and sensory processing.\n\n--- chunk 15 (source: ..\\data\\html\\asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html) ---\nAutism spectrum disorder (ASD) is a neurodevelopmental condition associated with differences in social communication, restricted interests, repetitive behaviors, and sensory processing. Although ASD has received increasing clinical and public attention, the experience of being identified, diagnosed, and treated is not the same for all patients. Gender and racial factors shape who is recognized early, who is overlooked, who receives appropriate services, and who must overcome greater barriers before support becomes available. These disparities matter because delayed identification can postpone therapy, school accommodations, family guidance, and access to specialized care.\n\n--- chunk 16 (source: ..\\data\\html\\asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html) ---\nReducing disparities among ASD patients requires action at multiple levels. Screening tools and clinical training should reflect a broader range of autistic presentations, especially in girls and culturally diverse populations. Providers should be trained to recognize masking, subtle social communication differences, and the ways autism may be expressed differently across communities. Diagnostic pathways should include interpreters, culturally responsive family communication, and patient navigation support for families facing practical barriers.\n\n--- chunk 17 (source: ..\\data\\html\\asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html) ---\nGirls with ASD may present differently from the traditional diagnostic picture that was developed largely from male-centered samples. In many cases, girls show fewer overt repetitive behaviors, stronger superficial social imitation, or interests that appear socially acceptable and therefore do not immediately raise concern. Some girls also engage in “masking” or “camouflaging,” meaning they consciously or unconsciously hide autistic traits in order to fit social expectations. As a result, they may be identified later, misdiagnosed with anxiety or mood disorders first, or never formally diagnosed at all.\n\n--- chunk 18 (source: ..\\data\\html\\asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html) ---\nThe most overlooked patients are often those who sit at the intersection of both kinds of disadvantage, such as autistic girls from Black, Hispanic, Indigenous, or other underserved communities. These patients may be less likely to fit dominant stereotypes of autism and more likely to encounter structural barriers in healthcare and education. A clinician who expects autism to appear mainly in boys, and who also carries cultural assumptions about family communication or behavior, may miss the diagnosis entirely.\n\n--- chunk 19 (source: ..\\data\\html\\asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html) ---\nAutism spectrum disorder (ASD) is a neurodevelopmental condition associated with differences in social communication, restricted interests, repetitive behaviors, and sensory processing. Although ASD has received increasing clinical and public attention, the experience of being identified, diagnosed, and treated is not the same for all patients. Gender and racial factors shape who is recognized early, who is overlooked, who receives appropriate services, and who must overcome greater barriers before support becomes available. These disparities matter because delayed identification can postpone therapy, school accommodations, family guidance, and access to specialized care.\n\n--- chunk 20 (source: ..\\data\\html\\asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html) ---\nOne of the most persistent disparities in autism is the gap between boys and girls in diagnosis and recognition. Recent CDC surveillance data reported that ASD was identified far more often among boys than girls, with prevalence among boys about 3.4 times that among girls in the 2022 surveillance sample. While some biological and developmental differences may contribute to this pattern, many researchers argue that the gap also reflects underrecognition of autism in girls rather than only true differences in occurrence.\n\n--- chunk 21 (source: ..\\data\\html\\asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html) ---\nCenters for Disease Control and Prevention. Prevalence and Early Identification of Autism Spectrum Disorder Among Children Aged 8 Years — Autism and Developmental Disabilities Monitoring Network, 16 Sites, United States, 2022. CDC MMWR, 2025.\n\n--- chunk 22 (source: ..\\data\\html\\asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html) ---\nWiggins, L. D., et al. Disparities in Documented Diagnoses of Autism Spectrum Disorder Based on Demographic, Individual, and Service Factors. CDC / Autism Research, 2020.\n\n--- chunk 23 (source: ..\\data\\pdfs\\Autism spectrum disorder definition, epidemiology, causes, and clinical evaluation.pdf) ---\n11. de Giambattista C, Ventura P, Trerotoli P, et al. Subtyping the autism spectrum disorder: comparison of children with high functioning autism and asperger syndrome. J Autism Dev Disord 2019;49:138-50.\n\nYour job: for EACH chunk, decide whether it contains information that\ndirectly helps answer the ORIGINAL USER QUESTION.\n\nA chunk is RELEVANT only if a reader could extract at least one fact from\nit that contributes to answering the question. Tangential mentions of the\ntopic, generic background, or off-topic text are NOT relevant.\n\nThen give an OVERALL verdict:\n- "PASS"    : majority of chunks are relevant AND together they could plausibly support an answer.\n- "PARTIAL" : some chunks are relevant but coverage is thin or incomplete.\n- "FAIL"    : few or no chunks are relevant; the agent should retry with a different query.\n\nReturn ONLY a JSON object (no markdown, no prose, no code fences) with this exact shape:\n\n{\n  "verdict": "PASS" | "PARTIAL" | "FAIL",\n  "per_chunk": [\n    {"index": 0, "relevant": true,  "reason": "<one short sentence>"},\n    {"index": 1, "relevant": false, "reason": "<one short sentence>"}\n  ],\n  "overall_reason": "<one or two sentences>"\n}\n', 'role': 'system'}], 'model': 'llama-3.1-8b-instruct', 'max_completion_tokens': 1024, 'stream': False, 'temperature': 0.0}}
