s, culturally responsive family communication, and patient navigation support for families facing practical barriers [Source:../data/html/asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html].\n\n--- chunk 2 (source: ..\\data\\html\\asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html) ---\nDisparities in ASD cannot be understood through gender or race alone. Gender-based underrecognition, especially among girls, and racial inequities in diagnosis and care continue to shape who receives support and who is left behind. Although surveillance data suggest some progress in identification across racial and ethnic groups, significant inequities remain in timing, pathway, quality of diagnosis, and access to services. A fairer system will require clinicians, educators, and policymakers to move beyond one-size-fits-all assumptions and recognize the diverse ways autism appears across different populations.\n\nPROPOSED GROUPS:\nGroup 0:\n  chunk_index=0  sentence="Recent CDC surveillance data reported that ASD was identified far more often among boys than girls, with prevalence among boys about 3.4 times that among girls in the 2022 surveillance sample."\n  chunk_index=1  sentence="While some biological and developmental differences may contribute to this pattern, many researchers argue that the gap also reflects underrecognition of autism in girls rather than only true differences in occurrence."\n\nGroup 1:\n  chunk_index=0  sentence="Disparities in ASD cannot be understood through gender or race alone."\n  chunk_index=2  sentence="Disparities in ASD cannot be understood through gender or race alone."\n\nGroup 2:\n  chunk_index=0  sentence="Although surveillance data suggest some progress in identification across racial and ethnic groups, significant inequities remain in timing, pathway, quality of diagnosis, and access to services."\n  chunk_index=2  sentence="Although surveillance data suggest some progress in identification across racial and ethnic groups, significant inequities remain in timing, pathway, quality of diagnosis, and access to services."\n\nA group is CONFIRMED only if:\n- all sentences have the same subject\n- all sentences make the same claim\n- all sentences have the same meaning\n- removing duplicate copies would NOT lose unique information\n\nIMPORTANT:\n- Same topic is NOT enough.\n- Related ideas are NOT enough.\n- Similar wording is NOT enough.\n- Advice and explanation are NOT automatically the same fact.\n- General statements and specific instructions are NOT redundant.\n- If one sentence adds a unique detail, the group is REJECTED.\n- If the subjects are different real-world entities, the group is REJECTED.\n- If meanings conflict or one sentence negates another, the group is REJECTED.\n\nBe STRICT.\nBe CONSERVATIVE.\nIf unsure, REJECT the group.\n\nGOOD:\n"ASDs vary motor speed by controlling voltage and frequency."\n"An ASD regulates motor speed by adjusting frequency and voltage."\nVerdict: CONFIRMED\n\nBAD:\n"ASD patients may experience sensory overload."\n"Healthcare providers should reduce loud noises."\nVerdict: REJECTED\nReason: related topic, but different facts.\n\nBAD:\n"Managing ASD patients requires individualized care."\n"Reduce bright lights and loud sounds."\nVerdict: REJECTED\nReason: general guidance versus specific instruction.\n\nBAD:\n"ASDs reduce energy costs and mechanical wear."\n"ASDs reduce energy costs."\nVerdict: REJECTED\nReason: one sentence contains unique information.\n\nOUTPUT FORMAT:\nReturn ONLY a JSON array.\nOne object per proposed group, in the same order as the input groups.\n\n[\n  {\n    "group_index": 0,\n    "verdict": "CONFIRMED",\n    "reason": "Same subject and same factual claim."\n  },\n  {\n    "group_index": 1,\n    "verdict": "REJECTED",\n    "reason": "Related topic but different facts."\n  }\n]\n\nBAD OUTPUTS:\n- Python code\n- Pseudocode\n- Markdown\n- Explanations outside JSON\n- Text before or after the JSON array\n\nThe first character of your response must be \'[\'.\nYour respon