l/asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html]. Diagnostic pathways should include interpreters, culturally responsive family communication, and patient navigation support for families facing practical barriers [Source:../data/html/asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html].\n\n[5] A fairer system will require clinicians, educators, and policymakers to move beyond one-size-fits-all assumptions and recognize the diverse ways autism appears across different populations.\n\n[6] The most overlooked patients are often those who sit at the intersection of both kinds of disadvantage, such as autistic girls from Black, Hispanic, Indigenous, or other underserved communities [Source:../data/html/asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html] [Source:../data/html/asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html]. These patients may be less likely to fit dominant stereotypes of autism and more likely to encounter structural barriers in healthcare and education [Source:../data/html/asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html] [Source:../data/html/asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html]. A clinician who expects autism to appear mainly in boys, and who also carries cultural assumptions about family communication or behavior, may miss the diagnosis entirely [Source:../data/html/asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html] [Source:../data/html/asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html].\n\n[7] Girls with ASD may present differently from the traditional diagnostic picture that was developed largely from male-centered samples. In many cases, girls show fewer overt repetitive behaviors, stronger superficial social imitation, or interests that appear socially acceptable and therefore do not immediately raise concern.\n\nDRAFT ANSWER (working material — not the source of truth):\nMasking in the context of autism refers to the behavior of hiding autistic traits to fit social expectations. This can lead to delayed or missed diagnoses, as individuals may be identified later or misdiagnosed with anxiety or mood disorders. Girls 