riteria for ASD diagnosis [Source:../data/pdfs/Autism spectrum disorder definition, epidemiology, causes, and clinical evaluation.pdf] [Source:../data/pdfs/Autism spectrum disorder definition, epidemiology, causes, and clinical evaluation.pdf]. There are varying reports estimating the extent of and effects of this change. One study found that with parental report of ASD symptoms alone, the DSM-5 criteria identified 91% of children with clinical DSM-IV PDD diagnoses [Source:../data/pdfs/Autism spectrum disorder definition, epidemiology, causes, and clinical evaluation.pdf] [Source:../data/pdfs/Autism spectrum disorder definition, epidemiology, causes, and clinical evaluation.pdf]. However, a systematic review suggests only 50% to 75% of children with clinical DSM-IV PDD diagnoses would be identified by the DSM-5 criteria, not just "50% to 75% of"\n\n[5] The World Health Organization (WHO) estimates the international prevalence of ASD at 0.76% [Source:../data/pdfs/Autism spectrum disorder definition, epidemiology, causes, and clinical evaluation.pdf] [Source:../data/pdfs/Autism spectrum disorder definition, epidemiology, causes, and clinical evaluation.pdf]. The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) estimates about 1.68% of United States (US) children aged 8 years (or 1 in 59 children) are diagnosed with ASD [Source:../data/pdfs/Autism spectrum disorder definition, epidemiology, causes, and clinical evaluation.pdf] [Source:../data/pdfs/Autism spectrum disorder definition, epidemiology, causes, and clinical evaluation.pdf]. In the US, parent-reported ASD diagnoses in 2016 averaged slightly higher at 2.5% [Source:../data/pdfs/Autism spectrum disorder definition, epidemiology, causes, and clinical evaluation.pdf] [Source:../data/pdfs/Autism spectrum disorder definition, epidemiology, causes, and clinical evaluation.pdf]. The prevalence of ASD in the US more than doubled between 2000–2002 and 2010–2012 according to Autism and Developmental Disabilities Monitoring Network (ADDM) estimates [Source:../data/pdfs/Autism spectrum disorder definition, epidemiology, causes, and clinical evaluation.pdf] [Source:../data/pdfs/Autism spectrum disorder definition, epidemiology, causes, and clinical evaluation.pdf]. Although it may be too early to comment on trends, in the US, the prevalence of ASD has appeared to stabilize with no statistically significant increase from 2014 to 2016 [Source:../data/pdfs/Autism spectrum disorder definition, epidemiology, causes, and clinical evaluation.pdf] [Source:../data/pdfs/Autism spectrum disorder definition, epidemiology, causes, and clinical evaluation.pdf]. Changing diagnostic criteria may impact prevalence and the full impact of the DSM-5 diagnostic criteria has yet to be seen [Source:../data/pdfs/Autism spectrum disorder definition, epidemiology, causes, and clinical evaluation.pdf] [Source:../data/pdfs/Autism spectrum disorder definition, epidemiology, causes, and clinical evaluation.pdf].\n\n[6] Additionally, primary care clinicians need to be aware of (and evaluate for) potential co-occurring conditions in children with ASD [Source:../data/pdfs/Autism spectrum disorder definition, epidemiology, causes, and clinical evaluation.pdf]. According to a surveillance study of over 2,000 children with ASD, 83% had an additional developmental diagnosis, 10% had at least one psychiatric [Source:../data/pdfs/Autism spectrum disorder definition, epidemiology, causes, and clinical evaluation.pdf].\n\n[7] for a definitive diagnosis and comprehensive neuropsychological assessment. Children with ASD should also be screened for common co-morbid diagnoses. While no clear biomarkers or diagnostic measures exist, clinical genetic testing is recommended as part of the initial medical evaluation. Further medical work up or subspecialist referrals may be pursued based on specific patient characteristics [Source: "../data/pdfs/Autism spectrum disorder definition, epidemiology, causes, and clinical evaluation.pdf"]