2026-07-14 19:07:53 | DEBUG    | nodes.generate_draft | [7b5fb05e-bcd3-4de9-aa4c-e4e4ee8d756e] | [GENERATE_DRAFT] draft:
As a pediatrician, it is essential to be aware of the new definition of Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD), which aims to be more accurate and diagnose ASD at an earlier age [Source: Autism spectrum disorder definition, epidemiology, causes, and clinical evaluation.pdf]. However, studies have predicted a decrease in ASD prevalence due to the change from the DSM-IV to the DSM-5 [Source: Autism spectrum disorder definition, epidemiology, causes, and clinical evaluation.pdf]. 

When evaluating children for ASD, primary care clinicians should consider sensory needs during examinations or procedures to improve outcomes and quality of life for individuals with ASD [Source: learned_qa]. A comprehensive assessment should include a complete physical exam, including assessment for dysmorphic features, a full neurologic examination with head circumference, and a lamp examination of the skin [Source: learned_qa].

Co-occurring conditions in ASD include anxiety, attention deficit/hyperactivity disorder (ADHD), obsessive compulsive disorder, and mood disorders or other disruptive behavior disorders [Source: learned_qa]. In children with ASD, the rate of any anxiety disorder was found to be similar at 42%, with 46% having oppositional defiant disorder and 8% having mood disorders [Source: learned_qa].

Primary care clinicians are recommended to refer children to early intervention if less than 3 years of age or to the public school system for psychoeducational evaluation in order to establish an individual education program (IEP) if the child is three years of age or older [Source: learned_qa].
